specified in Theorem \ref{th:main}. 

Let us note that $\mathcal{S}(\Z_1\times \Z_n,{\bf 0})$ is a permutation solution. On the other hand,  $n=1$ forces $|G|=1$ and $\mathcal{S}(\Z_1\times \Z_1,0)$ is a trivial one element solution. Hence, from now on, we can assume $n>1$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:dis00}
Let $(X,\sigma,\tau)=\mathcal{S}(G\times \Z_n,{\bf c})$ and let $\Psi\colon \dis{X}\to G$  be defined by $\prod L_{(0,i)}^{p_i}\mapsto \sum p_i\cdot c_i$. Then the mapping
$\Psi$ is a well-defined epimorphism and
$\Ker\Psi=\dis{X}_{(0,0)}$.
In particular, $(G,+,0)\cong\dis{X}/\dis{X}_{(0,0)}$.
%The group $(G,+,0)$ of the solution $\mathcal{S}(G\times \Z_n,{\bf c},f)$ is isomorphic to the quotient group $\dis{G\times \Z_n}/\dis{G\times \Z_n}_{(0,0)}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Recall, the abelian group  $\dis{X}$ is generated by the set $\{L_{(0,i)} \mid i\in\Z_n\}$ and the group $(G,+,0)$ is generated by the set ~$\{c_{i}\mid i\in\Z_n\}$. Since $L_{(0,i)}((a,0))=(a+c_i,0)$, for all $a\in G$ and $i\in\N$, the mapping $\Psi$ can be viewed as
the action of~$\dis{X}$ on $G\times\{0\}$ and therefore it is a homomorphism from $\dis{X}$ onto $G$.
%Moreover, for each $(a,0)\in G\times \Z_n$ there is $\gamma\in\dis{X}$ with $(a,0)=\gamma((0,0))$. By construction this means that the mapping $\Phi$ is onto. 
Clearly $\Ker\Psi=\dis{X}_{(0,0)}$ and hence $(G,+,0)\cong\dis{X}/\dis{X}_{(0,0)}$.
\end{proof}

