Recall, the solution is indecomposable, if the permutation group
$\mathcal{G}(X)$ is transitive. 
Let us have $a\in G$ arbitrary. We first  prove that
$(a +c_i,0)$ is in the same orbit as $(a,0)$, for any $i\in \Z_n$:
\[
\sigma_{(0,i)}\sigma_{(0,0)}^{-1}((a,0))
\stackrel{\eqref{dis}}{=}
L_{(0,i)}((a,0))=
(a+c_{i},0).
\]
By an induction argument then $(a,0)$ lies in the same orbit as $(0,0)$, for any $a\in G$. 
Now, for any $b\in G$ and $j\in\Z_n$,
\[ \sigma_{(0,0)}^j ((b,0))=\pi^j((b,0))=(b,j)\]
