We will denote the solution described above by $\mathcal{S}(G\times \Z_n,{\bf c})$, for $n\in \mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$, a group $(G,+,0)$ and ${\bf c}\in G^n$ 
specified in Theorem \ref{th:main}. 
Let us note that $\mathcal{S}(\Z_1\times \Z_n,{\bf 0})$ is a permutation solution. On the other hand,  $n=1$ forces $|G|=1$ and $\mathcal{S}(\Z_1\times \Z_1,0)$ is a trivial one element solution. Hence, from now on, we can assume $n>1$.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:dis00}
Let $(X,\sigma,\tau)=\mathcal{S}(G\times \Z_n,{\bf c})$ and let $\Psi\colon \dis{X}\to G$  be defined by $\prod L_{(0,i)}^{p_i}\mapsto \sum p_i\cdot c_i$. Then the mapping
$\Psi$ is a well-defined epimorphism and
$\Ker\Psi=\dis{X}_{(0,0)}$.

